Package: prisonpc-bad-package-conflicts-everyone
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
 prisonpc-ersatz-gpg (= ${binary:Version}),
 prisonpc-ersatz-logrotate (= ${binary:Version}),
 prisonpc-ersatz-kio (= ${binary:Version}),
 prisonpc-ersatz-parted (= ${binary:Version}),
 prisonpc-ersatz-dictionaries-common (= ${binary:Version}),
Architecture: all
Description: conflict with CANTHAPPEN packages
 Some packages should NEVER be installed (e.g. terminal emulators).
 Sometimes updates cause apt to start installing such a package.
 When this happens, the build should abort, so a human can investigate.
 .
 Note that this is different from a prisonpc-ersatz-* package.
 Ersatz packages are where we HAVE DECIDED the best option is to lie to apt.
 This package is for things where we HAVEN'T DECIDED yet.
 .
 One easy way to test for this is to ask for task-xfce-desktop, which
 should fail due to a conflict relating to X terminal emulators.
 .
 Most bad packages can be banned for everyone (this package).
 A few things inmates MUST NOT have, but staff MUST have.
 That is done in prisonpc-bad-package-conflicts-inmates.
Conflicts:
# NOTE: apt_preferences(5) can block src:X; deb Conflicts cannot.
#       deb Conflicts can block all provides of a virtual package; apt_preferences(5) cannot.
#       Therefore we block e.g. src:gnupg2 in apt preferences, and
#       we block e.g. cron-daemon (provided by cron|bcron|cronie|systemd-cron) here.
#
# UPDATE: doing as much as possible in apt_preferences(5) had problems:
#          • EVERY apt query, even things like "apt show mg", took ~10s.
#            This substantially slowed down the build, because
#            even though mmdebstrap installs everything in one run,
#            we do lots of things like is-marble-rebuild-needed.
#          • error messages on conflicts often give the too cause – but not always.
#          • it was possible to install negatively-pinned packages in certain edge cases.
#         Therefore I'm going back to doing a "pre-compiled" list in here, but
#         I will script it more, so that e.g. "all shells except bash and dash"
#         will be just a one-liner you re-run to get the exact output to paste in.
#         The obvious downsides to doing it in here are:
#          • you cannot easily comment out a single entry for an ad-hoc run
#          • you cannot block src:foo (unless you manually expand it out in a script).
#            e.g. we write the output of "apt list ?source-package(foo)" here,
#            it blocks libfoo-1 by name, but then backports gets libfoo-2.
#            apt_preferences would automatically block it without needing a manual update.
#            This is RELATIVELY rare though.
#            (A notable exception is linux-source-N.M &c which churn a lot, and
#            have no Provides virtual package to conflict on here.)
#
## Metapackages – can NEVER block these via apt_preferences(5)
## ===========================================================
# Manually selected packages
 ssh-askpass, dhcpd, editor, cron-daemon, httpd, httpd-cgi, httpd-wsgi, irc, ircd, automaken, c-shell,
## Inmates MUST NOT have unnecessary crypto
## ========================================
# Notes:
#  • apt → sqv (not gpgv) nowadays
#  • sqv → src:nettle
#  • libreoffice-core → libgpgme → gpg (or gpg-from-sq).
#    Worked around by our prisonpc-ersatz-gpg.
#  • Provides: pinentry always comes from src:pinentry, but
#    Provides: ssh-askpass is spread over multiple src: packages.
#    Therefore prisonpc-ersatz can block the latter better than we can.
#    Neither actually provide crypto, just GUI helpers.
#    ssh-askpass-gnome is src:openssh; staff need parts of src:openssh.
#  • src:gcr4 et al are Gnome password manager.
#    gvfs → gvfs-libs → libgcr4 (src:gcr4)
#    chromium → libcups → libgnutls → libp11-kit0 (src:p11-kit)
#    gvfs, msmtp → libsecret (src:libsecret)
#  • src:kf6-kwallet et al are KDE password manager.
#  • src:qca2 is some Qt5/6 crypto thing.
#  • zip/7zip/rar formats support AES (strong crypto).
#    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZIP_(file_format)#Encryption
#    Deny the ones we can.
#    As at Debian 11, src:zip DOES NOT support AES.
#    libarchive (bsdtar) DOES support AES.
#    vlc-plugin-base & gvfs-backends → libarchive
#  • libreoffice CANNOT do zip-level AES on .docx/.odt/&c, but
#    libreoffice CAN do docx/odt level encryption inside the zip.
#    We nerf that elsewhere, in libreoffice XML config.
